1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,652
Aarav slowly turns up
the supply on a JFET.

2
00:00:03,952 --> 00:00:08,563
At first, the current climbs with
the voltage, like a normal resistor.

3
00:00:08,863 --> 00:00:10,414
Then it stops climbing.

4
00:00:10,414 --> 00:00:12,248
More voltage, same current.

5
00:00:12,548 --> 00:00:14,102
Why does it go flat?

6
00:00:14,102 --> 00:00:16,599
Today we read the JFET's curves.

7
00:00:17,099 --> 00:00:21,132
Think of a soft hose again,
with water pushed in at one end.

8
00:00:21,482 --> 00:00:24,053
Push a little harder,
and more water flows.

9
00:00:24,403 --> 00:00:28,006
But a hard push also pinches
the hose near the far end.

10
00:00:28,356 --> 00:00:30,032
Harder push, tighter pinch.

11
00:00:30,032 --> 00:00:32,305
So the flow settles at a steady value.

12
00:00:32,955 --> 00:00:37,999
In a JFET, the push is
the drain voltage, VDS.

13
00:00:38,349 --> 00:00:40,769
It pinches the drain end of the channel.

14
00:00:40,769 --> 00:00:42,365
So the current goes flat.

15
00:00:43,015 --> 00:00:45,026
Let's measure it on the breadboard.

16
00:00:45,376 --> 00:00:47,672
The gate is set at zero volts.

17
00:00:47,672 --> 00:00:49,482
We slowly raise VDS.

18
00:00:49,832 --> 00:00:53,477
From zero, the drain current
rises almost in a straight line.

19
00:00:53,827 --> 00:00:56,238
Here the channel acts like a resistor.

20
00:00:56,238 --> 00:00:57,927
This is the ohmic region.

21
00:00:58,277 --> 00:01:00,995
At four volts, the drain end pinches.

22
00:01:00,995 --> 00:01:03,671
This is VP, the pinch off voltage.

23
00:01:04,021 --> 00:01:06,791
After that, the current
stays near ten milliamperes.

24
00:01:06,791 --> 00:01:08,100
We call it IDSS.

25
00:01:08,450 --> 00:01:12,662
This flat part is the saturation,
or constant current, region.

26
00:01:13,012 --> 00:01:16,570
Now set the gate to minus
one volt, and sweep again.

27
00:01:16,920 --> 00:01:19,166
The curve goes flat earlier, and lower.

28
00:01:19,166 --> 00:01:21,024
About five point six milliamperes.

29
00:01:21,374 --> 00:01:23,633
At minus two volts, it is lower still.

30
00:01:23,633 --> 00:01:25,197
Two point five milliamperes.

31
00:01:25,547 --> 00:01:28,771
Push VDS too far, and
the current shoots up.

32
00:01:28,771 --> 00:01:30,066
That is breakdown.

33
00:01:30,066 --> 00:01:30,966
Avoid it.

34
00:01:31,515 --> 00:01:35,767
Now take the flat values, and plot
them against the gate voltage.

35
00:01:36,117 --> 00:01:38,383
This is the transfer characteristic.

36
00:01:38,383 --> 00:01:40,321
It is a curve, a parabola.

37
00:01:40,671 --> 00:01:45,148
At zero gate volts, ID
is IDSS, its top value.

38
00:01:45,498 --> 00:01:49,501
At VGS off, minus four volts, ID is zero.

39
00:01:49,851 --> 00:01:51,668
The curve follows Shockley's equation.

40
00:01:52,018 --> 00:01:58,009
ID equals IDSS, times one
minus VGS over VP, all squared.

41
00:01:58,709 --> 00:02:02,256
Notice, VP and VGS off have the same size.

42
00:02:02,256 --> 00:02:03,730
Here, four volts.

43
00:02:04,380 --> 00:02:06,606
Past pinch off, the current stays flat.

44
00:02:06,606 --> 00:02:08,513
That is where a FET amplifies.

45
00:02:09,313 --> 00:02:11,873
Three numbers describe a JFET.

46
00:02:11,873 --> 00:02:13,143
Let's meet them.

47
00:02:13,493 --> 00:02:14,393
One.

48
00:02:14,062 --> 00:02:15,949
Drain resistance, r d.

49
00:02:15,949 --> 00:02:19,018
Change in VDS, over change in ID.

50
00:02:19,718 --> 00:02:21,758
We keep the gate voltage fixed.

51
00:02:21,758 --> 00:02:24,006
On the flat part, r d is large.

52
00:02:24,356 --> 00:02:25,256
Two.

53
00:02:24,873 --> 00:02:26,526
Transconductance, g m.

54
00:02:26,526 --> 00:02:29,315
Change in ID, over change in VGS.

55
00:02:30,015 --> 00:02:31,608
We keep VDS fixed.

56
00:02:31,608 --> 00:02:34,199
Its unit is siemens, also called mho.

57
00:02:34,549 --> 00:02:35,449
Three.

58
00:02:35,242 --> 00:02:37,265
Amplification factor, mu.

59
00:02:37,265 --> 00:02:40,572
Change in VDS, over change in VGS.

60
00:02:41,272 --> 00:02:42,942
We keep ID fixed.

61
00:02:42,942 --> 00:02:44,371
Mu has no unit.

62
00:02:44,721 --> 00:02:48,244
Now multiply and divide
mu by the change in ID.

63
00:02:48,944 --> 00:02:54,588
Mu becomes delta VDS over delta
ID, times delta ID over delta VGS.

64
00:02:55,288 --> 00:02:57,210
The first part is r d.

65
00:02:57,210 --> 00:02:58,840
The second is g m.

66
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:01,178
So mu equals r d times g m.

67
00:03:01,828 --> 00:03:03,156
Worked example.

68
00:03:03,156 --> 00:03:05,313
Find r d, g m and mu.

69
00:03:05,663 --> 00:03:09,715
With the gate fixed, VDS
rises from four to ten volts.

70
00:03:10,065 --> 00:03:12,624
ID rises from eight to eight
point two milliamperes.

71
00:03:12,974 --> 00:03:16,402
So r d is six volts over
zero point two milliamperes.

72
00:03:16,402 --> 00:03:17,634
Thirty kilo ohms.

73
00:03:18,334 --> 00:03:22,576
With VDS fixed, the gate
changes by zero point two volts.

74
00:03:22,926 --> 00:03:25,246
ID changes by zero point six milliamperes.

75
00:03:25,946 --> 00:03:29,147
So g m is zero point
six over zero point two.

76
00:03:29,147 --> 00:03:30,595
Three milli siemens.

77
00:03:31,295 --> 00:03:34,533
Mu is thirty kilo ohms
times three milli siemens.

78
00:03:34,533 --> 00:03:35,711
That is ninety.

79
00:03:36,361 --> 00:03:37,823
Now, the exam trap.

80
00:03:38,173 --> 00:03:40,634
Many students think the current
becomes zero at pinch off.

81
00:03:40,984 --> 00:03:42,009
It does not.

82
00:03:42,009 --> 00:03:45,849
At pinch off, ID stops rising
and stays nearly constant.

83
00:03:46,499 --> 00:03:49,998
ID becomes zero only when
the gate reaches VGS off.

84
00:03:50,348 --> 00:03:51,969
Where is this asked?

85
00:03:51,969 --> 00:03:53,829
In PUC board papers.

86
00:03:54,479 --> 00:03:59,984
Draw the curves, define r d, g m and
mu, and prove mu equals r d times

87
00:03:59,984 --> 00:04:00,884
g m.

88
00:04:01,278 --> 00:04:02,178
Try this.

89
00:04:02,145 --> 00:04:07,253
AJFET has mu of three hundred,
and r d of one hundred kilo ohms.

90
00:04:07,253 --> 00:04:11,568
With VDS fixed, the gate
changes by zero point one volts.

91
00:04:11,568 --> 00:04:13,535
How much does ID change?

92
00:04:18,935 --> 00:04:19,835
B.

93
00:04:19,193 --> 00:04:20,441
Zero point three milliamperes.

94
00:04:20,791 --> 00:04:23,447
First, g m equals mu over r d.

95
00:04:23,797 --> 00:04:27,562
Three hundred over one hundred
kilo ohms is three milli siemens.

96
00:04:27,912 --> 00:04:31,583
Change in ID is g m times change in VGS.

97
00:04:31,933 --> 00:04:34,400
Three milli siemens times
zero point one volts.

98
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:36,410
That is zero point three milliamperes.

99
00:04:37,060 --> 00:04:38,606
Screenshot this card.

100
00:04:41,806 --> 00:04:42,965
Next, the MOSFET.

101
00:04:42,965 --> 00:04:45,123
A gate that never touches the channel.
